[Music]  this morning we're looking at how some  police departments are moving forward  with reforms on their own despite the  breakdown of congressional efforts to  overhaul policing in the 16 months since  the murder of george floyd more than  1300 u.s  people have been killed by police  officers chris vancleave rode along with  a special police car stocked with less  lethal tools to help officers avoid  deadly confrontation  i'm sending a team with a pepper ball  gun police respond to a suicidal man in  june he's got a knife outside the train  station in aurora illinois the officers  called for backup from a social worker  less lethal and after about an hour what  could have been deadly ended peacefully  we're not afraid to try new things keith  cross is aurora's chief of police the  27-year veteran runs the second largest  city police department in illinois the  uniform can be a barrier to someone  who's in crisis and they don't see the  social worker as a threat it's been very  very helpful cross became chief 14  months after george floyd was killed by  minneapolis police officer derek chovin  floyd's death and the protests that  ignited across the country led to  demands for fundamental changes in  policing is policing broken there's no  question that police needs to change in  some very dramatic ways all the arrests  are made georgetown law professor  christie lopez developed de-escalation  training now used by at least 175 police  departments in the u.s and canada a lot  of state and local jurisdictions have  realized they need to step up and they  need to be the ones to lead change is  all it was is arguing we've got to train  officers to be guardians not warriors to  de-escalate rather than escalate force  to prevent other officers from  committing misconduct or causing harm  got the knife  for chief cross that better training is  the key they're going to play like they  practice and so they're going to take  things slow if they can they're going to  try and de-escalate we're going to use  the least amount of force that we have  to use just go ahead drop that knife for  me okay the department just started  using this immersive virtual reality  system got one down it can simulate the  toughest things cops face we're going to  get you the help you need like how to  deal with their own implicit bias when  to use force and when to back away we  can teach the officer to not jump to the  lethal level we got some time if we got  some space on our side let's use that  and if we can end this thing at a  non-lethal level  that's a win-win for everybody involved  we tried the system sir i'd like to see  your hands  my hands are right here and it becomes  very real very fast sir stay in the car  sir stay in the car my response during  this simulated traffic stop was to draw  my gun when the driver unexpectedly got  out of the car  the trainer said that was too early and  could have provoked what happened  moments later when the driver pulled out  a gun whoa sir put the gun down  put the gun down  it becomes real it becomes real and it's  good to watch officers go through that  to see how they react to that come on  just do it for me drop the knife you  really do get a sense for how quickly  you have to decide what you're going to  do  we spent a friday night shift riding  with officer wesley boyer he's driving  one of the department's 16 ll squad cars  the initials stand for less lethal the  units carry things officers can use  instead of their sidearms a gun that  shoots pepper balls and another firing  hard foam projectiles  what's the benefit to having less lethal  cars the more options the more decisions  you can make you could prevent an  officer from having to use deadly floors  a recent survey showed 90 percent of  americans supported more training for  police and departments nationwide are  making changes in june the nypd the  nation's largest police department  announced all 35 thousand officers will  get new de-escalation training  berkeley california decided to shift  enforcement of minor traffic violations  like expired registration to unarmed  transportation department workers  and in san francisco changing training  and policies led to almost 70 percent  fewer instances of use of force by  officers you don't have to go hands on  you can use your voice and your presence  tracy mccrary is a lieutenant in san  francisco police union officer she says  there are always cops who see change as  handcuffs but she sees george floyd's  death as a game changer and that's  changed the face of policing and either  you're going to get on board with that  or this isn't the profession for you  nationally departments have seen  retirements jump by 45 percent and  resignations spike by 18 percent while  the officers staying on the beat are  being asked to keep the peace  while keeping pace with change for cbs  mornings i'm chris van cleave in aurora  illinois  it looks like they're on to something in  aurora but you have to have buy-in from  the police officers too and you don't  always it's interesting when the chief  there says you know get with it or get  out and then you see that nationally a  lot of people are retiring and or  resigning yeah it's good to see those  less lethal vehicles  but i do have family members that work  as police officers they tell me about  these confrontations and they get  intense like that yeah but as long as  they have de-escalation de-escalation  tactics i think they'll have less deaths  you know they have seconds to respond  seconds make a decision very tough it is  a very difficult job

